ute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the determinant of\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & z \\\\ z & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis $-48$, find all the possible values of $z.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013865/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4d9f2d260fa3f739857d232731bcd3159a45e84744b703b6868b58f255c6dbd" "basis" 1176 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013865/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & z \\\\ z & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & z \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}2 & z \\\\ z & 1\\end{aligned}|+5|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ z & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(3+z)-(2-{z}^{2})+5(-2-3z) \\\\  & =6+2z-2+{z}^{2}-10-15z \\\\  & ={z}^{2}-13z-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-48,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-13z-6 & =-48 \\\\ {z}^{2}-13z+42 & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{-(-13)\\pm \\sqrt{{13}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 42}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pm \\sqrt{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pm 1}{2} \\\\  & =6,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=6$ and $z=7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013865/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4d9f2d260fa3f739857d232731bcd3159a45e84744b703b6868b58f255c6dbd" "basis" 1176 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013865/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013865/activity-